= The Camel, The Lion, And the Child


== The lion
Challenge your parents, your institutions, your beliefs. If you're religious,
consider, in your mind, what it means for you to  _not_ be religious!

One possibility of challenging your beliefs is that you might find, that _some
of your beliefs_ actually serve you! That's ok, keep those. But you,
uncomfortably, might also find that some other beliefs _are keeping you stuck
and unhappy_. It is those beliefs that we must challenge. 

The lion is needed to challenge the dragon because the lion is _angry_. We, as
"nice, modern" people aren't allowed to be angry, at least in the public
workplace, or to our loved ones, or even to ourselves. We fear the destructive
anger that is used in a way that hurts others. So we keep the anger
inside until it grows and grows and grows and it explodes and causes us great
pain and turmoil. But I want to say, you are allowed to be angry, and not nice.
If it serves you to not be nice, be an asshole! If you, for some
reason, don't like someone, write those colorful words down on a piece of paper
or to a clanker. You might find that the anger is actually trying to _tell you
something_, that a belief or social group or your family, is _not serving your
needs in some way_, and that should _move you in a direction that allows you to
resolve that conflict_. The anger is important in challenging unhelpful
beliefs, if you use it well!

This might seem easy to say but this process takes a long time. I had to have
an _actual person_ tell me to "be angry" and still I denied that I was an angry
person!


== The child
Can you imagine _your child_ being evil? Perhaps, not well adjusted, but not
_evil_!
